{:edb/db-before-t 2246 :edb/db-after-t 2247 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535559 50 #inst "2026-10-14T12:26:44.579Z" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186059670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517098 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186059670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517102 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186059670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517103 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186059670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186517119 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517118 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517098 1071 "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517098 1072 #uuid "0012d1f3-80aa-5b68-8b2b-0c91a3fcab4f" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517098 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right.\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,1).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1+2,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,1+1)=(1,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(1,2).$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517099 1080 "(1,2)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517099 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517099 1083 #uuid "0fe06a3e-823d-57fa-9df0-511990a5ce48" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517100 1080 "(-4,4)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517100 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517100 1083 #uuid "1393eb73-99fe-5f7e-ba74-d0adba541d6e" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517101 1080 "(0,0)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517101 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517101 1083 #uuid "2265c6d2-b222-552a-a01c-467b94c9f34c" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517109 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517102 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5)?$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517102 1072 #uuid "26d65a2e-856c-5dbc-a557-be478c1e06a4" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517102 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=4f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right.\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,2),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,2).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+5,2)=(7,2).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by stretching the graph by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(7,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(7,4\\cdot (2))=(7,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5)$ is $(7,8).$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517114 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517103 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1)?$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517103 1072 #uuid "3098f894-dc2f-57e8-ace9-9f4b9aa94b09" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517103 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left.\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-1,-4)=(-3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by stretching the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,2\\cdot (-4))=(-3,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1)$ is $(-3,-8).$" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517104 1080 "(-3,2)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517104 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517104 1083 #uuid "324cd10e-6861-5be6-94ad-7b198d22049f" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517105 1080 "(-3,\\frac{1}{2})" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517105 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517105 1083 #uuid "44daafcd-40e6-532a-89aa-ac6f698763a9" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517106 1080 "(7,\\frac{1}{2})" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517106 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517106 1083 #uuid "50040d57-2b2c-562b-9b76-6080dd9806ec" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517107 1080 "(-3,-8)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517107 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517107 1083 #uuid "5a6ac256-8320-5b6e-ac63-eca3134d05fa" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517108 1080 "(7,8)" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517108 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517108 1083 #uuid "6ee6e2bd-00c4-5a7c-9db4-f2403dc480cf" 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517105 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517106 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517108 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517111 13194139535559 17592186045443 true] [17592186517109 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517117 13194139535559 175921860454